.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt ZFS-EVENTS 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-events
.Nd display file-level event history for ZFS datasets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zfs
.Cm events
.Op Fl c
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Nm zfs
.Cm events
.Op Fl j
.Op Fl n Ar count
.Op Fl o Ar object
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Op Ar path
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm zfs Cm events
command displays the file-level event history for a ZFS dataset.
Events are logged when files and directories are created, modified, renamed,
or deleted, providing a complete audit trail of filesystem changes.
.Pp
Events are stored in an internal ring buffer within each dataset and are
keyed by ZFS object ID, ensuring that the history of a file is preserved
even after renames.
The event log is included in ZFS send streams and travels with the pool,
making it useful for replication conflict detection and forensic analysis.
.Pp
To enable event logging on a dataset, set the
.Sy events
property:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs set events=on tank/data
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Sy events
property is inheritable, so enabling it on a parent dataset will enable
event logging for all descendant datasets.
.Pp
Event logs are per-dataset and are not part of a snapshot's data:
arguments containing a snapshot delimiter
.Pq Sq @
are rejected.
.
.Ss Event Types
The following event types are recorded:
.Bl -tag -width "TRUNCATE" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy CREATE
File or directory created
.It Sy REMOVE
File or directory deleted
.It Sy RENAME
File or directory renamed (includes old and new names)
.It Sy LINK
Hard link created
.It Sy SYMLINK
Symbolic link created
.It Sy TRUNCATE
File truncated
.It Sy SETATTR
File attributes changed (permissions, ownership, times)
.It Sy WRITE
File content written (requires
.Sy events_io Ns = Ns Sy on )
.It Sy READ
File content read (requires
.Sy events_io Ns = Ns Sy on )
.El
.Pp
.Sy WRITE
and
.Sy READ
events are recorded only when the
.Sy events_io
property is set to
.Sy on ;
see
.Sx IO AUDITING
below.
.
.Sh IO AUDITING
In addition to the metadata operations above,
.Nm zfs Cm events
can record file content access as
.Sy WRITE
and
.Sy READ
events.
IO auditing is disabled by default and is enabled per dataset:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs set events_io=on tank/data
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Sy events
property must be set to
.Sy on
for IO auditing to have an effect.
Each
.Sy WRITE
or
.Sy READ
record carries
.Sy io_offset
and
.Sy io_bytes
in addition to the common fields; in JSON output they appear as
.Sy io_offset
and
.Sy io_bytes
members.
.Pp
IO events are coalesced by a per-file, per-direction time fence
controlled by the
.Sy events_io_window
property (milliseconds, 0 to 3600000, default 1000).
While the window is open, I/O to the same file in the same direction
is absorbed into a single pending record: byte counts are summed into
.Sy io_bytes ,
and
.Sy io_offset
is the window's first offset.
Bytes are coalesced, never dropped: every byte written or read while
the window is open appears in exactly one record's
.Sy io_bytes .
The window closes on the next I/O past the window, on any non-IO
event for the same file (records keep cause order, so a
.Sy WRITE
followed by a
.Sy RENAME
is emitted in that order), and on
.Xr close 2 .
.Pp
The
.Sy uid
and
.Sy gid
of a coalesced record are those of the caller whose I/O triggered
emission; windows containing I/O from multiple writers are
attributed to the emitter.
Setting
.Sy events_io_window=0
disables the fence and records every
.Xr write 2
and
.Xr read 2
call individually.
.Pp
A
.Sy WRITE
record carries the transaction group of the write's final
transaction.
A
.Sy READ
record carries the pool's open transaction group at the time of the
read; reads create no transaction of their own, so this value is a
commit-timeline anchor, not a claim that the read was part of that
transaction group.
.Pp
Limitations:
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
Writes performed through
.Xr mmap 2
bypass the VFS read/write paths and generate no IO events.
.It
At
.Sy events_io_window=0 ,
sustained bulk I/O emits a record per syscall (roughly 1024 records
per GiB with 1M block size) and can wrap a small event ring
continuously; size
.Sy events_size
accordingly or use a nonzero window, which emits at most one record
per window period.
.El
.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-o object"
.It Fl c
Clear the dataset's event log instead of displaying it, then exit.
The dataset must be mounted.
.Fl c
cannot be combined with
.Fl j ,
.Fl n ,
.Fl o ,
or a
.Ar path
argument.
Clearing preserves ring identity: consumers tracking the ring GUID
(e.g.\&
.Xr zmetad 8 )
see the same ring instance, reset to empty.
.It Fl j
Output in JSON format for machine parsing.
All events are printed as a single JSON array; each event is one
object on its own line.
See
.Sx OUTPUT
for the exact key set.
.It Fl n Ar count
Limit output to the oldest
.Ar count
events in the ring buffer.
By default, all events in the ring buffer are displayed.
.Ar count
must be a positive integer; zero and non-numeric values are rejected.
.It Fl o Ar object
Display only events for the specified ZFS object ID.
Object IDs can be obtained from
.Xr zdb 8
or from previous
.Nm zfs Cm events
output.
The value must be numeric.
.It Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
The dataset to query for events.
.It Ar path
Optional dataset-relative path used to filter events.
The path is resolved against the dataset's mountpoint and converted
to an object ID, so the query shows the full history of that object,
under any name it has had.
The file must currently exist: a name that was renamed away can no
longer be resolved and must be queried by object ID
.Pq Fl o
instead.
.El
.
.Sh OUTPUT
In the default (non-JSON) mode a header is printed followed by one
line per event with the following columns:
.Bl -tag -width "OPERATION" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy TXG
Transaction group in which the event occurred
.It Sy OBJECT
ZFS object ID of the affected file
.It Sy OPERATION
Type of event (CREATE, REMOVE, RENAME, SETATTR, TRUNCATE, WRITE,
READ, \&...)
.It Sy NAME
Dataset-relative name of the affected file, for operations that
carry a name.
RENAME lines append the previous name in parentheses
.Pq Dq "(from oldname)" .
.El
.Pp
The kernel record also carries a timestamp, uid, gid, and mode where
applicable, but these are not part of the text or JSON output of this
command; use the wire interface
.Pq Xr zmetad 8
for the full record.
.Pp
In JSON mode
.Pq Fl j
the output is a single JSON array.
Each event object always contains the keys
.Sy txg ,
.Sy object ,
and
.Sy op .
The keys
.Sy name
and
.Sy parent
appear only when the record carries them (name-bearing metadata
operations); TRUNCATE, SETATTR, WRITE, and READ records carry
neither.
Operation-specific keys are added as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "TRUNCATE" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy RENAME
.Sy old_name , old_parent
.It Sy TRUNCATE
.Sy old_size , new_size
.It Sy SYMLINK
.Sy target
.It Sy WRITE , READ
.Sy io_offset
(the window's first offset) and
.Sy io_bytes
(the summed byte total for the window)
.El
.Pp
An empty log prints
.Dq no events found .
If records were overwritten by ring wraparound, a message of the form
.Dq N record(s) lost to log wraparound
is printed to standard error.
If the log cannot be read at all,
.Nm zfs Cm events
prints a diagnostic to standard error and exits 1.
.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "XX"
.It 0
Successful query or clear.
.It 1
Invalid option or argument, snapshot argument, unopenable dataset,
failed clear, or a first-page read error.
.El
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1: Viewing the events of a dataset
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events tank/data
TXG        OBJECT   OPERATION  NAME
---------- -------- ---------- --------------------
1847       128      CREATE     newfile.txt
1848       128      SETATTR
1850       128      RENAME     renamed.txt (from newfile.txt)
1852       129      CREATE     another.txt
1855       128      REMOVE     renamed.txt
.Ed
.Ss Example 2: Getting the history of a specific file
The path argument is resolved to the file's object ID, so the whole
object history is shown, including records logged under earlier
names:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events tank/data important.txt
TXG        OBJECT   OPERATION  NAME
---------- -------- ---------- --------------------
1200       256      CREATE     draft.txt
1205       256      SETATTR
1340       256      RENAME     important.txt (from draft.txt)
.Ed
.Ss Example 3: JSON output for scripting
.Fl n
returns the oldest events in the ring:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events -j -n 2 tank/data
[{"txg":1847,"object":128,"op":"CREATE","name":"newfile.txt","parent":4},
{"txg":1848,"object":128,"op":"SETATTR"}]
.Ed
.Ss Example 4: IO auditing
WRITE and READ records carry no name; correlate them by object ID:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs set events_io=on tank/data
# zfs set events_io_window=5000 tank/data
# dd if=/dev/zero of=/tank/data/blob bs=1M count=64
# zfs events -j tank/data
[{"txg":1860,"object":130,"op":"CREATE","name":"blob","parent":4},
{"txg":1861,"object":130,"op":"WRITE","io_offset":0,"io_bytes":67108864}]
.Ed
.Ss Example 5: Clearing a dataset's event log
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs events -c tank/data
cleared event log for 'tank/data'
.Ed
.
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
.Sy Evolving .
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-diff 8 ,
.Xr zfs-set 8 ,
.Xr zfsprops 7 ,
.Xr zmetad 8 ,
.Xr zpool-features 7
.
.Sh NOTES
The event log is stored as a ring buffer within the dataset.
When the buffer is full, the oldest events are overwritten.
The size of the buffer is controlled by the
.Sy events_size
property, which defaults to 1 MiB.
.Pp
Events are recorded within the same transaction group as the operation
that triggered them, ensuring consistency with snapshots.
.Pp
The
.Sy org.openzfs:events
pool feature must be enabled for event logging to function.
The feature is activated when the first event is logged on any
dataset in the pool, not at the moment the
.Sy events
property is set.
.Pp
For long-term event retention beyond the ring buffer capacity, use
.Xr zmetad 8
to export events to an external SQLite database.
